    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="2" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="17.3" heatingNighttime="17.3" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is pretty air tight, however there are a few nuisance areas that can be addressed. 



Air leakage was observed through both bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model.



Air leakage was also observed around the light fixture and window frame in the back right bedroom (when looking from the front of the house). Both of these areas can be sealed up using interior caulking as we discussed. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 20&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1.0 inches of polyisocyanurate insulation. I have modelled adding an additional 1.0 inch (R6) of polyisocyanurate insulation.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an additional R6 of polyisocyanurate insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation. This includes adding 2 inches of insulation to the lower 'crawlspace' as well. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1.0 inches of polyisocyanurate insulation. Same as the main wall of the home, I have modelled adding an additional 1.0 inch (R6) of polyisocyanurate insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the concrete walls, and an additional inch (R6) rigid foam polyisocyanurate insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,450&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;53.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of your windows appear to be in good shape, but do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

As your home is fairly airtight, there appears to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Current Oil Boiler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



You currently have a 170 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.



You currently have a loop installed in which the boiler is used to heat the hot water inside the electrical hot water tank. You mentioned the boiler is shutdown during the summer months (assumed to be 3 months of the year), however it would be beneficial to leave the boiler on year round, and simply turn down the thermostats throughout the home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;95% AFUE Propane Boiler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



When the time comes to replace your oil fired boiler, and if you decide to install a 95%AFUE propane boiler, the same setup can be applied in using the boiler to heat your hot water tank. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using a 95% AFUE propane boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you decide to install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, as well as a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings (with current oil furnace):&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing a ULC Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 800 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler.

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Combustion spillage - &gt;5Pas - Atmospherically Vented Appliances&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (exhaust fans, clothes dryer, range hood, etc.), indicated a pressure drop of&lt;b&gt; 9.9 PascalÂs&lt;/b&gt;. Because this number is higher than &lt;b&gt;5.0 PascalÂs&lt;/b&gt;, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliance(s) since you have atmospherically vented boiler.



&lt;b&gt;We strongly recommend that you consult an HVAC specialist and install a carbon monoxide (CO) detector in your home, as well as a smoke detector on each floor, if not already present.&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

